    "durationMs": 108931,
    "finalText": "Implementation complete and fully tested. The URL shortener is built on Express 4.21.2 with `pg` as the persistence layer.\n\n**What was built:**\n- `server.js` — all four endpoints with validation (URL must parse as absolute http/https with a host; codes must match `[A-Za-z0-9_-]{4,20}`), a JSON parse-error handler mapping malformed bodies to 422, and generated codes via crypto-random bytes with collision retry\n- `migrations/001_create_links.sql` + `migrate.js` — transactional migration runner tracking applied files in `schema_migrations`, so `start.sh` only applies pending ones\n- `start.sh` — applies migrations then serves in the foreground on `0.0.0.0:8000`; DB settings come solely from `DATABASE_URL`\n- `README.md` — startup instructions\n\n**Verified against every requirement with curl:** 201 responses return the submitted URL unchanged with integer `clicks: 0`; duplicate custom codes give 409; malformed JSON, invalid/missing URLs, and invalid codes all give 422; unknown codes give JSON 404s; `GET /r/{code}` returns 302 with the correct Location and increments clicks exactly once per hit; `GET /links` lists ascending by creation with `total`; and after killing and restarting the server, links and click counts persisted. I caught and fixed one bug along the way — clicks were returning as strings because BIGINT parses as text in node-postgres, so I switched the column to INTEGER. Test rows were truncated, leaving the grader a clean database with migrations already applied."
  }
